    "summary": "High‑energy set highlighted by soaring Dark Star and tight The Eleven",
    "blurb": "The show delivers an expansive Dark Star that flows into Saint Stephen and a masterful The Eleven, capped off with a spirited second‑set finish featuring Cosmic Charlie. Standout moments include Mama Tried, Hard To Handle, and the crisp rhythm of The Eleven. While source quality varies, excellent audience recordings capture the performance clearly.",
    "review": "Thelma Theater on December 11, 1969 finds the Dead deep in their early‑Dead groove, mixing R&B swagger with psychedelic stretch. The first set builds from tight country‑rock numbers like Mama Tried and Hard To Handle into a long Dark Star that soars over Saint Stephen before launching into an audacious The Eleven – Phil’s bass gymnastics and Jerry’s fluid leads keep the momentum alive despite the looser pacing of late ’69 shows. The second set continues the journey with heartfelt renditions of Morning Dew and Next Time You See Me, then rolls through a soulful stretch of Good Lovin’ and Dancin' In The Street before the classic sandwich of Cryptical Envelopment → Drums → The Other One → Cryptical Envelopment, ending on an uplifting Cosmic Charlie. Listeners will appreciate the relaxed vibe and inventive improvisations, especially when heard on the crystal‑clear audience tapes that preserve the band’s chemistry without the hiss of the poorer soundboard source.",
